.TH DRIVEUTILITY-WIPE "8" "August 2026" "driveutility" "System Administration Commands"

.SH NAME
driveutility-wipe \- erase a storage device

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B driveutility-wipe
.BI -d " device_path"
.RI [ -m " method" ]
.RI [ -p " passes" ]
.RI [ -t " type" ]
.RI [ -z ]
.RI [ -s " size_mb" ]
.RI [ -b " block_size" ]

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B driveutility-wipe
erases a whole storage device using a controller-native erase method, discard/TRIM, or a traditional host-side overwrite. The target device is unmounted before any destructive operation begins.
.PP
For SSDs, controller-native secure erase or discard avoids the full-device host writes caused by zero or random overwrites. Regular discard is intended for quickly resetting a device and is not a substitute for secure erase when data must be made unrecoverable.
.PP
For NVMe secure erase, the current LBA format, metadata-transfer mode, and protection-information settings are preserved. Operations that may implicitly affect other namespaces are rejected unless Identify Controller reports exactly one namespace and exactly one namespace is visible to the kernel.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B -d, --device
Specify the whole block device to erase, for example
.I /dev/sdb
or
.I /dev/nvme0n1.
This option is required.

.TP
.B -m, --method
Select the erase method. This option takes precedence over
.BR --type .
Supported methods are:
.RS
.TP
.B secure
Choose a controller-native secure erase automatically. NVMe uses Cryptographic Erase when supported and User Data Erase otherwise. ATA drives use the UDisks2 ATA Secure Erase interface. If neither is available, secure discard may be attempted when supported.
.TP
.B discard
Issue discard/TRIM for the whole device without overwriting every logical block from the host. This is fast and reduces unnecessary SSD writes, but is not guaranteed to make data unrecoverable.
.TP
.B secure-discard
Request secure discard through
.BR blkdiscard --secure .
The device must explicitly support secure discard.
.TP
.B nvme-user
Force NVMe User Data Erase (SES=1).
.TP
.B nvme-crypto
Force NVMe Cryptographic Erase (SES=2). The controller must advertise cryptographic erase support.
.TP
.B ata-secure
Force standard ATA Secure Erase through UDisks2.
.TP
.B ata-enhanced
Force ATA Enhanced Secure Erase through UDisks2.
.TP
.B zero
Overwrite the target with zeros.
.TP
.B random
Overwrite the target with random data.
.RE

.TP
.B -t, --type
Legacy overwrite selector. Accepted values are
.B zero
and
.BR random .
It is used only when
.B --method
is not specified. The default is
.BR zero .

.TP
.B -p, --passes
Number of overwrite passes. This applies only to the
.B zero
and
.B random
methods. The default is 1.

.TP
.B -z, --final-zero
After a random overwrite, add one final zero-fill pass. This option applies only to the
.B random
method.

.TP
.B -s, --size
Overwrite only the first specified number of megabytes. This option applies only to host-side overwrite methods. Controller erase and discard methods always operate on the whole selected device.

.TP
.B -b, --block-size
Block size passed to
.B dd
for host-side overwrites. The default is
.BR 1M .

.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The device was erased successfully.
.TP
.B 1
General error, including an invalid target device or failure of the destructive operation.
.TP
.B 2
Argument parsing error.
.TP
.B 4
The selected erase method is unavailable or unsupported by the target drive.

.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B Automatically choose the best controller secure erase method:
.B driveutility-wipe -d /dev/nvme0n1 -m secure

.TP
.B Discard all blocks on an SSD without a full overwrite:
.B driveutility-wipe -d /dev/sdb -m discard

.TP
.B Force NVMe cryptographic erase:
.B driveutility-wipe -d /dev/nvme0n1 -m nvme-crypto

.TP
.B Perform 3 random overwrite passes followed by zeros:
.B driveutility-wipe -d /dev/sdb -p 3 -t random -z

.SH NOTES
ATA Secure Erase can be unavailable while the drive security state is frozen. In that case, Drive Utility does not attempt to bypass the frozen state automatically.
.PP
Multiple overwrite passes are retained for compatibility, but they are generally inappropriate for SSDs because flash translation layers and wear leveling prevent direct control over physical NAND placement.

